But it does point to the fact that it isn't a low percentage play. If I
played every character this way and only 1 in 5 got to minetown and got
protection, I'd quickly abandon the strategy. But it doesn't happen if you
work it fully.

>The thing is that without it you can be much more successful; the key
>to high percentage play in Nethack is getting rid of sheer random
>chances for death and keeping you in the position where you have a
>chance to think about every threat and deal with it.

The problem is that leveling up increases the chances of dying. Each
character acquires attributes that helps them deal with that. But when you
get it out of balance, you often lose. So you have to learn how to gage
when to acquire extra attributes vs. the consequences for doing so.

Note that you are not saying not to play the protection racket. So you
agree that extra weightless AC is a benefit to a nethack character. You're
saying the smarter play is to level up. But it's hard to gauge the breakeven
point for the benefit of leveling up vs. its consequences. However it's a
much easier decision on leveling up the pet vs. any consequences, as there
are none.

There are these types of decision points throughout the game. For example
I never ever dip in fountains. I find the benefit is small vs. the potential
risks. Of course I could use that early wish too.

